    <tr>
        <td><b>R2: Angular Distortion in Distance Calculations</b></td>
        <td>High</td>
        <td>Critical</td>
        <td>Discarded degree-based grids; projected all routing planes into physical metric UTM zones (42N–47N).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>R3: Heap Memory Exhaustion on Large Rasters</b></td>
        <td>Moderate</td>
        <td>High</td>
        <td>Implemented Two-Tier Lazy Loading (only load active bounding boxes into heap; evict unused presets).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>R4: Combinatorial Explosion in Constrained A*</b></td>
        <td>Moderate</td>
        <td>Moderate</td>
        <td>Formulated Lagrangian Multipliers with monotonic step pruning instead of raw Pareto frontier search.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>R5: Endpoints Dropped in Infeasible/Blocked Zones</b></td>
        <td>High</td>
        <td>High</td>
        <td>Implemented Smart Snapping with BFS vector-shift to nearest navigable metric cell.</td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2>1.5 Scope and Limitations</h2>
<p><b>Scope Boundary:</b> MARG produces preliminary highway corridor planning centerlines, Right-of-Way (RoW) acquisition reservations, interchange locations, feeder spur corridors, and cross-drainage/structural crossing inventories.</p>
<p><b>Non-Goals & Limitations:</b></p>
<ul>
    <li>Generated corridors do not constitute construction-ready 3D geometric designs (e.g., vertical spline curve transitions, superelevation designs, or digital mass-haul cut/fill earthwork balancing).</li>
    <li>Land-use boundaries and building footprints reflect remote sensing imagery and OSM datasets, which must be supplemented by ground-truth Cadastral (<i>Khasra/Khatauni</i>) revenue boundary surveys before legal land acquisition notices are published.</li>
    <li>Structural spans and interchange layouts represent strategic recommendations for cost modeling, subject to detailed site geotechnical bore-hole logs and structural engineering design.</li>
</ul>

<h1>Chapter 2: Literature Survey</h1>

<h2>2.1 Critical Review</h2>
<p>The science of automated highway alignment optimization has transitioned through three major eras over the past four decades:</p>

<h3>1. Traditional Geometric & Dynamic Programming Approaches (1980s–1990s)</h3>
<p>Early computer-aided highway design tools utilized dynamic programming, state-space branch-and-bound, or calculus-of-variations methods (e.g., Chew et al., 1989; Nicholson, 1973). These systems operated on heavily simplified digital terrain models (DTMs) with a singular objective: minimizing earthwork cut-and-fill volume while satisfying maximum grade constraints. They lacked the ability to process polygonal land-use boundaries, demographic clusters, or statutory environmental constraints.</p>

<h3>2. Genetic Algorithms (GAs) and Population-Based Heuristics (2000s–2010s)</h3>
<p>To address the non-linear, multi-modal nature of 3D alignment, researchers shifted toward evolutionary algorithms (e.g., Jha and Schonfeld, 2004; Kang et al., 2012; Jong and Schonfeld, 2003). These models represented alignments as B-spline curves or sequences of intersection points, evaluating populations via multi-objective fitness functions combining construction cost, land acquisition, and vehicle operating costs. While effective for continuous geometric optimization over small geographic extents, evolutionary algorithms scale poorly to regional scales (&gt; 50 km). They suffer from slow stochastic convergence, lack deterministic reproducibility, and frequently generate alignments that cut obliquely across linear environmental barriers due to coarse spatial discretization.</p>

<h3>3. Modern GIS-MCDA & Grid-Based Graph Pathfinding (2015–Present)</h3>
<p>Recent paradigms combine Geographic Information Systems Multi-Criteria Decision Analysis (GIS-MCDA) with graph search algorithms such as Dijkstra or A* (e.g., Effat and Hassan, 2013; Pushkarev et al., 2018). These approaches rasterize multiple thematic layers into a single composite impedance surface. However, existing implementations exhibit critical gaps: most operate in unprojected geographic coordinates (latitude/longitude degrees), introducing longitudinal metric distortions exceeding 15% across Indian latitudes; they evaluate isolated point alignments rather than contiguous corridor footprints, ignoring lateral Right-of-Way buffers (45 m–60 m); and they omit network connectivity (interchanges and feeder spurs), treating the expressway as a sealed pipe.</p>

<h2>2.2 Summary Table</h2>
<p class="table-caption">Table 2.1: Comparative Analysis of Highway Alignment Methodologies</p>
<table>
    <tr>
        <th>System / Authors</th>
        <th>Methodology</th>
        <th>Datasets Used</th>
        <th>Cost & Objective Formulation</th>
        <th>Spatial Resolution & Scale</th>
        <th>Key Limitations</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>Nicholson (1973); Chew et al. (1989)</b></td>
        <td>Dynamic Programming & Calculus of Variations</td>
        <td>Coarse DEM, uniform soil profiles</td>
        <td>Monolithic: Earthwork volume + pavement cost</td>
        <td>500 m grid; Local (&lt; 20 km)</td>
        <td>No environmental or land-use data; strictly 2D/simplified vertical alignment.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>Jong & Schonfeld (2003); Jha & Schonfeld (2004)</b></td>
        <td>Genetic Algorithms (GA) with B-spline representations</td>
        <td>USGS DEM, simplified GIS land-value maps</td>
        <td>Bi-criterion: Total capital expenditure vs travel time</td>
        <td>Continuous vectors; Small corridors (20–30 km)</td>
        <td>High stochasticity; long convergence times; lacks deterministic Pareto boundaries.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>Kang, Jha & Schonfeld (2012)</b></td>
        <td>Multi-Objective Genetic Algorithm (NSGA-II)</td>
        <td>GIS parcel layers, DEM, wetland vector boundaries</td>
        <td>Multi-objective: Construction cost, environmental damage, user cost</td>
        <td>Continuous; Sub-regional (40 km)</td>
        <td>Severe computational bottleneck on regional rasters; ignores utility/power-line infrastructure.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>Trimble Quantm / Bentley OpenRoads (Commercial)</b></td>
        <td>Proprietary Corridor Optimization Engine</td>
        <td>High-res LiDAR, CAD geometry, user cost zones</td>
        <td>Proprietary Cost Optimization (Earthwork, Structures, Property)</td>
        <td>Proprietary variable mesh; Corridor scale</td>
        <td>"Black-box" proprietary weighting; prohibitive licensing; lacks Indian statutory clearance integration.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>Pushkarev et al. (2018)</b></td>
        <td>Least Cost Path (LCP) & Dijkstra on Raster Grids</td>
        <td>Multi-spectral Landsat, SRTM DEM</td>
        <td>Weighted Linear Combination (WLC) of impedance rasters</td>
        <td>100 m grid; Regional</td>
        <td>Ignores physical length budgets; no interchange placement or secondary network planning.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>MARG (This Work)</b></td>
        <td><b>Metric A* with Lagrangian Relaxation + Post-Mainline Network Planner</b></td>
        <td><b>ISRO Bhuvan LULC, CartoDEM, OSM, 780+ India Districts, WII Sanctuaries</b></td>
        <td><b>Concurrent 13-Layer Multi-Criteria Cost Surface + 4-Pillar Trade-Off Profile</b></td>
        <td><b>250 m metric grid (UTM 42N–47N); Regional (&gt; 100 km)</b></td>
        <td><b>Overcomes angular distortion, enforces length caps, automates interchange/spur routing, checks statutory clearance.</b></td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2>2.3 Identification of Research Gaps</h2>
<p>From the literature, five critical research gaps were identified, which Project MARG directly resolves:</p>
<ol>
    <li><b>Lack of Geodesic Consistency in Least-Cost Routing:</b> Prior academic models calculate Euclidean distances on geographic coordinate systems (&Delta;lat, &Delta;lon). At Indian latitudes (8&deg;N to 36&deg;N), 1&deg; of longitude contracts from 110.57 km to 89.83 km, heavily skewing East-West path penalties relative to North-South paths.</li>
    <li><b>Omission of Physical Corridor-Length Constraints:</b> Least-Cost Path (LCP) algorithms produce serpentine alignments that wind excessively around low-cost patches, increasing total vehicle operating costs (VOC) and travel times beyond statutory IRC thresholds.</li>
    <li><b>Absence of Skew-Angle Penalty Modeling:</b> Real-world civil engineering costs depend strongly on crossing angles: an expressway crossing a major river, active fault, or railway line at a 30&deg; skew requires a bridge span approximately twice as long as an orthogonal (90&deg;) crossing. Prior grid algorithms do not penalize acute-angle crossings.</li>
    <li><b>Disconnection Between Mainline Pathfinding and Secondary Feeder Connectivity:</b> Existing systems treat the highway as an isolated point-to-point line, failing to model interchange feasibility or feeder connections to regional production centers and <i>mandis</i>.</li>
    <li><b>Absence of Automated Statutory Clearance Validation:</b> No existing platform cross-references alignment coordinates against legal boundaries of National Parks, Wildlife Sanctuaries, and Eco-Sensitive Zones (ESZs) to generate automated statutory clearance compliance reports.</li>
</ol>

<h1>Chapter 3: System Design & Methodology</h1>

<h2>3.1 Design Considerations</h2>
<p>Engineering a greenfield highway corridor requires balancing competing priorities across seven domains:</p>
<ul>
    <li><b>Economic Aspects:</b> Minimize lifecycle Capital Expenditure (CAPEX)—including civil earthwork, bridge spans, and land acquisition—while maximizing Operational Expenditure (OPEX) savings through reduced Vehicle Operating Costs (VOC) and shorter travel times.</li>
    <li><b>Environmental Aspects:</b> Maximize preservation of primary forest canopies, prevent bifurcation of contiguous wildlife sanctuaries, maintain natural surface drainage regimes, and protect perennial wetlands.</li>
    <li><b>Social Aspects:</b> Minimize physical displacement of rural and urban households, preserve heritage and religious structures, and prevent community severance across active settlements.</li>
    <li><b>Political & Regional Equity:</b> Ensure equitable regional connectivity by providing dedicated, grade-separated feeder spurs to underserved rural subdivisions and Sixth Schedule tribal areas.</li>
    <li><b>Ethical Considerations:</b> Maintain transparency in algorithmic decision-making. Trade-offs between environmental damage and project elongation must be mathematically quantified rather than hidden behind black-box heuristics.</li>
    <li><b>Health & Safety:</b> Steer alignments clear of active landslide scarps, seismic fault lineaments, and flood inundation zones; enforce IRC sight-distance and maximum gradient thresholds (&le; 5% for plain terrain, &le; 12% for interchanges).</li>
    <li><b>Sustainability & Climate Resilience:</b> Avoid 100-year flood inundation zones, protect riparian wetland buffer strips, and optimize alignments to lower fleet fuel consumption and carbon emissions.</li>
</ul>

<h2>3.2 Methodology</h2>

<h3>Overall System Architecture</h3>
