</div>

<h2>3.3 Modelling (Mathematical Formulations)</h2>

<h3>1. Geodesic Forward and Inverse Projections</h3>
<p>To eliminate degree distortion, geographic coordinates (&phi;, &lambda;) in WGS84 ellipsoidal space (semi-major axis a = 6378137.0 m, flattening f = 1/298.257223563) are mapped into local Universal Transverse Mercator (UTM) metric coordinates (X, Y) using the standard Transverse Mercator formulation:</p>

<div class="formula-box">
X = k<sub>0</sub> &middot; N &middot; [ A + (1 - T + C)(A<sup>3</sup>/6) + (5 - 18T + T<sup>2</sup> + 72C - 58e'<sup>2</sup>)(A<sup>5</sup>/120) ] + X<sub>0</sub>
<br><br>
Y = k<sub>0</sub> &middot; [ M - M<sub>0</sub> + N &middot; tan(&phi;) &middot; [ A<sup>2</sup>/2 + (5 - T + 9C + 4C<sup>2</sup>)(A<sup>4</sup>/24) + (61 - 58T + T<sup>2</sup> + 600C - 330e'<sup>2</sup>)(A<sup>6</sup>/720) ] ]
</div>

<p>Where k<sub>0</sub> = 0.9996, X<sub>0</sub> = 500,000 m (false easting), &lambda;<sub>0</sub> is the central meridian of the UTM zone, A = (&lambda; - &lambda;<sub>0</sub>)cos(&phi;), and N is the radius of curvature in the prime vertical.</p>

<h3>2. Multi-Layer Additive Cost Surface Formulation</h3>
<p>The continuous search space is discretized into an artificial metric graph G = (V, E) where nodes u, v &isin; V represent points separated by resolution &Delta;s = 250 m. The impedance cost of traversing an edge e = (u, v) is formulated as:</p>

<div class="formula-box">
C(u, v) = d(u, v) &middot; [ 1.0 + &sum;<sub>k=1..13</sub> ( w<sub>k</sub> &middot; p<sub>k</sub>(v) ) + &Omega;<sub>crossing</sub>(u, v) ]
</div>

<p>Where d(u, v) is physical Euclidean distance (250 m for orthogonal moves, 353.55 m for diagonal moves), p<sub>k</sub>(v) &ge; 0 is the dimensionless penalty contribution of the k-th spatial layer at node v, w<sub>k</sub> is the request-scoped user preference multiplier, and &Omega;<sub>crossing</sub>(u, v) is the geometric crossing penalty.</p>

<h3>3. Skew-Angle Crossing Penalty Model</h3>
<p>Civil engineering costs for crossing linear infrastructure (rivers, railways, active faults, and power corridors) increase inversely with the sine of the intersection angle &theta;<sub>intersect</sub>:</p>

<div class="formula-box">
&Omega;<sub>crossing</sub>(u, v) = &Omega;<sub>base</sub> &middot; [ 1.0 + &kappa;<sub>skew</sub> &middot; |cos(&theta;<sub>intersect</sub>)| ]
</div>

<p>An orthogonal crossing (&theta; = 90&deg;) yields &Omega;<sub>crossing</sub> = &Omega;<sub>base</sub>, while an acute crossing (&theta; &rarr; 0&deg;) penalizes parallel runs along river banks, fault scarps, or railway buffers.</p>

<h3>4. Metric A* Search with Admissible Octile Heuristic</h3>
<p>To guarantee optimal convergence on an 8-connected grid, MARG uses the metric Octile distance heuristic h(u, goal):</p>

<div class="formula-box">
h(u, goal) = &Delta;x<sub>metric</sub> + &Delta;y<sub>metric</sub> + (&radic;2 - 2) &middot; min(&Delta;x<sub>metric</sub>, &Delta;y<sub>metric</sub>)
</div>

<p>Where &Delta;x<sub>metric</sub> = |X<sub>u</sub> - X<sub>goal</sub>| and &Delta;y<sub>metric</sub> = |Y<sub>u</sub> - Y<sub>goal</sub>|. Since the minimum edge cost in an unpenalized cell is d(u, v), h(u, goal) &le; C*(u, goal), guaranteeing admissibility and optimal pathfinding.</p>

<h3>5. Constrained Elongation via Lagrangian Relaxation</h3>
<p>When a maximum route elongation constraint is imposed (Minimize C(&pi;) subject to L(&pi;) &le; L<sub>max</sub> = L<sub>baseline</sub> &times; [1 + &delta;<sub>max</sub>/100]):</p>

<div class="formula-box">
L(&pi;, &lambda;) = C(&pi;) + &lambda; &middot; (L(&pi;) - L<sub>max</sub>) &implies; C<sub>&lambda;</sub>(e) = C(e) + &lambda; &middot; d(e)
</div>

<p>As multiplier &lambda; increases across iterations, the algorithm penalizes physical detours, steering the corridor back toward the direct Euclidean baseline until L(&pi;<sub>&lambda;</sub>) &le; L<sub>max</sub>.</p>

<h3>6. Four-Pillar Scoring &amp; Composite Alignment Index</h3>
<p>Alignments are evaluated across four normalized pillars (Score &isin; [0, 100]):</p>
<ul>
    <li><b>Environmental Score (S<sub>env</sub>):</b> S<sub>env</sub> = max(5.0, 100.0 - [0.45&middot;A<sub>forest</sub> + 1.20&middot;A<sub>wetland</sub> + 0.02&middot;L<sub>bridge</sub> + 3.50&middot;N<sub>wildlife</sub> + 12.0&middot;L<sub>sanctuary</sub>])</li>
    <li><b>Social Safeguard Score (S<sub>soc</sub>):</b> S<sub>soc</sub> = max(5.0, 100.0 - [1.50&middot;P<sub>displaced</sub> + 2.20&middot;N<sub>buildings</sub> + 4.00&middot;N<sub>settlements</sub>])</li>
    <li><b>Directness &amp; Economy Score (S<sub>dir</sub>):</b> S<sub>dir</sub> = max(10.0, 100.0 - [2.50&middot;&delta;<sub>percent</sub> + 1.20&middot;N<sub>structures</sub>])</li>
    <li><b>Regional Connectivity Score (S<sub>conn</sub>):</b> S<sub>conn</sub> = min(100.0, 30.0 + 15.0&middot;N<sub>spurs</sub> + 10.0&middot;log<sub>10</sub>(P<sub>served</sub> + 1) - 0.50&middot;L<sub>spur</sub>)</li>
</ul>

<div class="formula-box">
Composite Alignment Index (CAI) = [ w<sub>env</sub>S<sub>env</sub> + w<sub>soc</sub>S<sub>soc</sub> + w<sub>dir</sub>S<sub>dir</sub> + 1.0&middot;S<sub>conn</sub> ] / [ w<sub>env</sub> + w<sub>soc</sub> + w<sub>dir</sub> + 1.0 ]
</div>

